  {
    "title": "Mako Bill to Improve Communication Regarding EPA Violations Passes House",
    "url": "https://www.repmako.com/News/38852/Press-Releases/Mako-Bill-to-Improve-Communication-Regarding-EPA-Violations-Passes-House",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Mako Bill to Improve Communication Regarding EPA Violations Passes House\nSeptember 30, 2026\nHARRISBURG – The House today approved legislation authored by Rep. Zach Mako (R-Lehigh/Northampton) to require timely notification of environmental violations at quarries and landfills.\nThe bill is the result of a several months-long lag in 2018 in notifying Whitehall Township and its residents that the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) issued a notice of non-compliance to a local landfill for high levels of PCBs in material accepted at the facility.\n“While I am encouraged that there have been no additional environmental violations at the quarry to my knowledge, I remain committed to advancing this legislation so residents can be confident they will be notified promptly of any incident that could affect their health or safety,” Mako said. “Quarry and landfill operators have a responsibility to be good neighbors to the communities around them, and this bill helps ensure that accountability. I am hopeful the Senate will act on this legislation promptly so delays in notification become a thing of the past.”\nHouse Bill 2229\nwould require the Pennsylvania Department of Environmental Protection to forward to municipalities within 14 days noncompliance notifications it receives from the EPA for violations of the Toxic Substances Control Act (TSCA) and Solid Waste Management Act (SWMA).\nThe bill now goes to the Senate for its consideration.\nThe 183rd House District includes Lowhill and North Whitehall townships and Slatington Borough in Lehigh County. It also consists of Allen, East Allen, Lehigh and Moore (part) townships, and Bath, North Catasauqua, Northampton and Walnutport boroughs in Northampton County.\nRepresentative Zachary Mako\n183rd Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Tracy Polovick\n717.260.6358\ntpolovick@pahousegop.com\nRepMako.com / Facebook.com/RepMako\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "The Pennsylvania House passed a bill requiring the state Department of Environmental Protection to notify municipalities within 14 days of receiving EPA noncompliance notices related to toxic substances and solid waste violations at quarries and landfills. The bill was introduced by Rep. Zach Mako and stems from a 2018 incident in which Whitehall Township residents experienced a months-long delay in learning about a PCB violation at a local landfill. The legislation now moves to the Senate for consideration.",
      "action_type": "bill_passed",
      "topics": [
        "environment",
        "public_safety"
      ],
      "spin_flag": false
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Mako to Host Shredding Event in Slatington",
    "url": "https://www.repmako.com/News/38807/Press-Releases/Mako-to-Host-Shredding-Event-in-Slatington",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Mako to Host Shredding Event in Slatington\nSeptember 24, 2026\nHARRISBURG\n– As a free service to the community, Rep. Zach Mako (R-Lehigh/Northampton) will co-host with Slatington Borough a shredding event to give residents of the 183rd Legislative District a chance to safeguard themselves against identity theft by having their personal documents securely destroyed.\nThe event will be held on Saturday, Oct. 3, from 10 a.m. to noon at the Robert “Bob” LaBold Maintenance Building,\n550 W. Church St\n.\n“Previous shredding events I’ve hosted have drawn a great turnout,” Mako said. “Unfortunately, identity theft remains a serious concern. Safely disposing of personal documents is an important way to protect yourself. If you have materials that need secure destruction, I hope you’ll join us.”\nAcceptable items include all paper and manila folders. No more than four bags or boxes per household are permitted, and no businesses, please. Staples or paper clips do not need to be removed. Examples of items that can be brought to the event include documents that contain personal information, including birth dates, Social Security numbers, bank account information and credit card numbers. Information also will be provided about consumer protection and identity theft at the events.\nFor more information, contact Mako’s offices at 610-502-2701 or 610-760-9805, or visit his website at\nRepMako.com\n.\nThe 183rd District includes Lowhill and North Whitehall townships and Slatington Borough in Lehigh County. It also consists of Allen, East Allen, Lehigh and Moore (part) townships, and Bath, North Catasauqua, Northampton and Walnutport boroughs in Northampton County.\nRepresentative Zachary Mako\n183rd Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Tracy Polovick\n717.260.6358\ntpolovick@pahousegop.com\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "State Representative Zach Mako is co-hosting a free document shredding event with Slatington Borough on October 3, 2026, from 10 a.m. to noon at a local maintenance building. Residents of the 183rd Legislative District can bring up to four bags or boxes of personal documents for secure destruction. The event aims to help residents reduce their risk of identity theft.",
      "action_type": "event",
      "topics": [
        "public_safety",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": false
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Municipalities in 183rd Awarded Grants, Mako Says",
    "url": "https://www.repmako.com/News/38682/Press-Releases/Municipalities-in-183rd-Awarded-Grants--Mako-Says",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Municipalities in 183rd Awarded Grants, Mako Says\nSeptember 15, 2026\nHARRISBURG – Rep. Zach Mako (R-Lehigh/Northampton) today announced he helped secure more than $2.5 million in Local Share Account (LSA) grants from the Commonwealth Financing Authority (CFA) for projects in the 183rd Legislative District.\nAct 71 of 2004 provides for the distribution of LSA gaming revenues through the CFA to support projects of public interest within the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania.\n“I’m pleased to partner with Sens. Nick Miller and Jarrett Coleman to secure funding for these important projects,” Mako said. “These grants will help support the repair and replacement of vital infrastructure and equipment municipalities and organizations depend on to serve our communities. I’m proud to help pursue this funding, which will make it possible to move these critical projects forward – projects that would have been much more difficult to complete without this support.”\nThose receiving LSA funding are:\n•\tBath Borough – $199,096 to purchase and install a backup generator to power three municipal buildings in the borough.\n•\tLehigh Township – $300,000 to purchase 45 portable and 20 mobile radios for municipal and emergency service use.\n•\tLehigh Township Municipal Authority – $925,119 to extend the water main for the proposed interconnection from the city of Bethlehem.\n•\tLehigh Valley Economic Investment Corporation – $100,000 to rehabilitate the Orchard Hills campus wastewater treatment facility of KidsPeace in North Whitehall Township.\n•\tLowhill Township – $100,000 to construct a salt shed.\n•\tNorth Catasauqua Borough – $350,000 to assist with the purchase of a new fire truck.\n•\tNorth Whitehall Township – $100,000 to assist with the purchase of a single-axle dump truck and accessories.\n•\tNorthampton Borough – $264,470 to purchase a street sweeper.\n•\tNorthampton Borough – $75,000 to assist with purchase of two new patrol vehicles for the police department.\n•\tWalnutport Borough – $100,000 to assist with the purchase of a new dump truck for the Public Works Department.\n•\tWalnutport Borough – $41,472 to purchase new police equipment, including tasers, vehicle cameras and body cameras.\nThe 183rd House District includes Lowhill and North Whitehall townships and Slatington Borough in Lehigh County. It also consists of Allen, East Allen, Lehigh and Moore (part) townships, and Bath, North Catasauqua, Northampton and Walnutport boroughs in Northampton County.\nRepresentative Zachary Mako\n183rd Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Tracy Polovick\n717.260.6358\ntpolovick@pahousegop.com\nRepMako.com / Facebook.com/RepMako\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "The Commonwealth Financing Authority approved over $2.5 million in Local Share Account grants for 11 projects across municipalities in Pennsylvania's 183rd Legislative District. The grants, funded by gaming revenues under a 2004 state law, will pay for equipment and infrastructure such as emergency radios, a fire truck, a water main extension, police vehicles, and a backup generator. Rep. Mako announced the awards alongside two state senators who also represent parts of the district.",
      "action_type": "other",
      "topics": [
        "infrastructure",
        "public_safety",
        "economy"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Mako to Host Small Games of Chance Seminar",
    "url": "https://www.repmako.com/News/38669/Press-Releases/Mako-to-Host-Small-Games-of-Chance-Seminar",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Mako to Host Small Games of Chance Seminar\nSeptember 14, 2026\nHARRISBURG – In an effort to provide the most up-to-date information to holders of small games of chance licenses, Rep. Zach Mako (R-Lehigh/Northampton) will host a Small Games of Chance Seminar.\nThe event will be held on Thursday, Sept. 24, from 6-8 p.m., at the Diamond Fire Company, 110 Main St., Walnutport.\n“Rules surrounding small games of chance can be confusing, even for those versed in the law,” Mako said. “I’m hopeful this seminar will clear up any misunderstandings and ensure organizations in the 183rd Legislative District have complete and correct information.”\nLiquor Code Enforcement Officer Derrick Devaney of the Bureau of Liquor Code Enforcement’s Compliance, Auditing and Gambling Enforcement Unit will lead the presentation. Time will be allotted for a question-and-answer session.\nRSVPs are strongly encouraged. To reserve a seat, please visit Mako’s website at\nRep.Mako.com\nor call one of his district offices at 610-502-2701 or 610-760-9805.\nThe 183rd District includes Lowhill and North Whitehall townships and Slatington Borough in Lehigh County. It also consists of Allen, East Allen, Lehigh and Moore (part) townships, and Bath, North Catasauqua, Northampton and Walnutport boroughs in Northampton County.\nRepresentative Zach Mako\n183rd Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Tracy Polovick\n717.260.6358\ntpolovick@pahousegop.com\nRepMako.com / Facebook.com/RepMako\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Rep. Zach Mako is organizing a free informational seminar on small games of chance licensing rules for organizations in his district. The event will be held on September 24, 2026, at the Diamond Fire Company in Walnutport, and will be led by a state Liquor Code Enforcement Officer. Attendees can ask questions and must RSVP in advance.",
