    "title": "Mako Announces School Safety Grants",
    "url": "https://www.repmako.com/News/38516/Press-Releases/Mako-Announces-School-Safety-Grants",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Mako Announces School Safety Grants\nAugust 17, 2026\nHARRISBURG –Rep. Zach Mako (R-Lehigh/Northampton) today announced more than $1.4 million in School Safety and Mental Health grants will be awarded to five school districts within the 183rd Legislative District.\nThe funding will be used to support physical security enhancements and/or mental health initiatives. It was renewed for the 2026-27 school year as part of the recently passed state budget.\n“Every child deserves to feel safe while at school.” Mako said. “This funding will help school districts enhance security, increase access to mental health resources and better prepare for potential safety threats. I’m proud to support this important and worthwhile program.”\nThose receiving grants are:\n•\tCatasauqua Area School District – $173,782.\n•\tNorthampton Area School District – $335,487.\n•\tNorthern Lehigh School District – $173,074.\n•\tNorthwestern Lehigh School District – $185,547.\n•\tParkland School District – $533,650.\nThe 183rd District includes Lowhill and North Whitehall townships and Slatington Borough in Lehigh County. It also consists of Allen, East Allen, Lehigh and Moore (part) townships, and Bath, North Catasauqua, Northampton and Walnutport boroughs in Northampton County.\nRepresentative Zach Mako\n183rd Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Tracy Polovick\n717.260.6358\ntpolovick@pahousegop.com\nRepMako.com / Facebook.com/RepMako\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Rep. Zach Mako announced that five school districts in his district will receive a combined $1.4 million in state grants for school safety and mental health programs. The grants were part of the recently passed state budget and will fund physical security upgrades and mental health resources. Mako did not author or sponsor this specific funding; he is announcing the distribution of grants to his district.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
