    "raw_text": "Northampton County Lawmakers Set the Record Straight on Budget Dispute with County Executive McClure\nSeptember 29, 2025\nHARRISBURG – Reps. Ann Flood (R-Northampton), Zach Mako (R-Lehigh/Northampton) and Milou Mackenzie (R-Lehigh/Northampton/Montgomery) today sent a letter to Northampton County Executive Lamont McClure correcting statements about Pennsylvania’s budget impasse.\nThe lawmakers pushed back against claims that Senate Republicans are responsible for the impasse, noting the Senate passed a stopgap budget on Aug. 12 to release funding while negotiations continued. Less than 24 hours later, House Democrats voted it down.\n“We urge you to stop playing politics with people's lives. Canceling events and sending letters intended to alarm constituents is not good government,” the letter states.\nThe letter also disputes a Sept. 19 letter from the county’s Department of Human Services, noting multiple inaccuracies and the omission of the stopgap budget. The lawmakers emphasized that 911 centers will not lose funding and encouraged McClure to urge House Democratic leadership and Speaker Joanna McClinton to convene the House to pass at least a stopgap budget and restore county program funding.\nA copy of the letter is available here\n.\nRepresentative Ann Flood\n138th Legislative District\nRepresentative Zachary Mako\n183rd Legislative District\nRepresentative Milou Mackenzie\n131st Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Will Jones\n717.260.6615\nWJones@pahousegop.com\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Three Republican state representatives sent a letter to Northampton County Executive Lamont McClure disputing his characterization of the Pennsylvania state budget impasse. They argue that Senate Republicans passed a temporary stopgap budget in August, which House Democrats rejected within 24 hours, and that McClure's communications to constituents contained inaccuracies. The lawmakers urged McClure to pressure House Democratic leadership to pass at least a temporary budget.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "economy",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
